{"name":"Organic","hex":"#747261","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"organic","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to or derived from living organisms. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Pertaining to an organ of the body of a living organism."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An organic compound.","An organic food."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/organic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Organism","blocks":[{"text":"An organism is any living thing that functions as an individual. Such a definition raises more problems than it solves, not least because the concept of an individual is also difficult. Several criteria, few of which are widely accepted, have been proposed to define what constitutes an organism. Among the most common is that an organism has autonomous reproduction, growth, and metabolism. This would exclude viruses, even though they evolve like organisms."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organism"},{"title":"Organ (biology)","blocks":[{"text":"In a multicellular organism, an organ is a collection of tissues joined in a structural unit to serve a common function. In the hierarchy of life, an organ lies between tissue and an organ system. Tissues are formed from same type cells to act together in a function. Tissues of different types combine to form an organ which has a specific function. The intestinal wall, for example, is formed by epithelial tissue and smooth muscle tissue. Two or more organs working together in the execution of a specific body function form an organ system, also called a biological system or body system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Internal_organs.png/330px-Internal_organs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organ_(biology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Basilikumwurzling.jpg/500px-Basilikumwurzling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Basilikumwurzling.jpg/1280px-Basilikumwurzling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Basilikumwurzling.jpg/500px-Basilikumwurzling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One criterion proposes that an organism cannot be divided without losing functionality.[6] This basil plant cutting is however developing new adventitious roots from a small bit of stem, forming a new plant.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasilikumwurzling.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/TMV_structure_simple.png/500px-TMV_structure_simple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/TMV_structure_simple.png/1280px-TMV_structure_simple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/TMV_structure_simple.png/500px-TMV_structure_simple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A virus such as tobacco mosaic virus is not a cell; it contains only its genetic material, and a protein coat.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATMV_structure_simple.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Apolemia_sp.jpg/500px-Apolemia_sp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Apolemia_sp.jpg/500px-Apolemia_sp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Apolemia, a colonial siphonophore that functions as a single individual","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApolemia_sp.jpg"},{"